From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winegstreamer: wm_reader header info, language list, packet size, timecode, playlist burn, sync reader output settings and frame ranges

The rest of the sync reader's Windows Media Format SDK objects:

- IWMHeaderInfo(2/3): the reader's header exposes the file attributes
  (Duration, Seekable) and none per stream: GetAttributeCount(Ex),
  GetAttributeByIndex(Ex), GetAttributeIndices and GetAttributeByName share
  one table and the SDK buffer protocol (NULL buffer gives the lengths,
  short buffer ASF_E_BUFFERTOOSMALL with the lengths, nothing written).
  Markers and scripts: none (count 0, index E_INVALIDARG). Codec info: one
  per stream, the FOURCC (video) or format tag (audio) as the codec
  information. Before Open every getter returns E_UNEXPECTED. The editing
  calls (SetAttribute, Add/Remove Marker/Script, Modify/Add/DeleteAttribute,
  AddCodecInfo) stay E_NOTIMPL: a reader's header is read-only (these belong
  to the metadata editor and the writer); the FIXMEs are dropped.
- IWMLanguageList: empty list; AddLanguageByRFC1766String E_NOTIMPL (reader).
- IWMPacketSize(2): Get Max/Min 3200 (the packet size the profile's
  expected packet count already uses), Set E_NOTIMPL (reader).
- IWMReaderTimecode: no timecode ranges (count 0, bounds E_INVALIDARG).
- IWMReaderPlaylistBurn: argument checks; Cancel / EndPlaylistBurn S_OK with
  nothing running, GetInitResults E_UNEXPECTED; InitPlaylistBurn stays
  E_NOTIMPL (one FIXME): burning runs the asynchronous reader, which is not
  this object.
- IWMSyncReader: Get/SetOutputSetting with the 16 documented settings, which
  differ for audio and video outputs (E_INVALIDARG / NS_E_INVALID_REQUEST /
  S_OK, defaults, BOOL/WORD/DWORD sizes, values kept per output, E_UNEXPECTED
  before Open); GetMaxOutputSampleSize; SetRangeByFrame / ByFrameEx
  (video streams only, NS_E_INVALID_REQUEST otherwise; start time = frame *
  frame duration); SetRangeByTimecode (NS_E_INVALID_REQUEST: no timecode).
- The QueryInterface FIXME on an unknown interface (a correct
  E_NOINTERFACE) becomes a WARN.

wmvcore's sync reader output settings test loses its todo_wine marks (the
output numbers it names are the Windows ones, audio 0 / video 1; the test
maps them to this reader's numbering as the asynchronous reader test does).

